WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.680
All right, friends.

00:04.680 --> 00:10.160
So now we come to the part where I'm going to show you how I usually use the set operators in my projects

00:10.160 --> 00:12.760
for data analysis or for data engineering.

00:12.920 --> 00:16.360
So here are the most important use cases for the set operators.

00:16.400 --> 00:16.760
All right.

00:16.800 --> 00:21.640
The first use case is combining similar tables before doing data analysis.

00:21.640 --> 00:27.320
In some scenarios we want to generate a report and we end up writing similar queries on top of similar

00:27.320 --> 00:28.000
tables.

00:28.240 --> 00:33.680
And we go at the end and join all the results from the queries in order to present the final reports.

00:33.680 --> 00:38.720
And now instead of doing that, what we can do first, we can go and combine all the similar informations

00:38.720 --> 00:45.120
into one table and then we can do on top of it a query, a data analysis in order to generate a report.

00:45.120 --> 00:48.040
And we can do that using the union or union all.

00:48.080 --> 00:49.360
Let's have few examples.

00:49.360 --> 00:53.920
So let's say that we have four tables employees customers suppliers and students.

00:53.920 --> 00:57.400
So as you can see all of them are sharing the same informations.

00:57.400 --> 00:59.650
They hold data about persons.

00:59.650 --> 01:05.170
So now let's say that you are generating a report that requires all the individuals in the organization

01:05.170 --> 01:06.170
in the database.

01:06.170 --> 01:11.810
So what you're going to end up doing is writing a SQL query for the employees, another one for customers

01:11.850 --> 01:14.810
and as well for the suppliers and the students.

01:14.810 --> 01:19.690
And then you're going to go and merge all the results from those queries into the final report.

01:19.730 --> 01:25.170
Now, the issue with this setup is that you are having a lot of queries, a lot of similar queries.

01:25.170 --> 01:26.930
So you have it here four times.

01:26.970 --> 01:31.810
And now what might happen is that you go and change the logic of the first two queries, and you forget

01:31.810 --> 01:37.490
later to do it for the other two, and you will get really inconsistent data in their reports.

01:37.490 --> 01:42.810
So instead of that, what we can do, we can go and use the set operators in order to combine first

01:43.050 --> 01:45.410
all those tables in one big table.

01:45.410 --> 01:50.170
So what we're going to do, we're going to go and use a union in order to combine those four tables

01:50.370 --> 01:52.050
into the table persons.

01:52.050 --> 01:53.450
So we're going to have it like this.

01:53.450 --> 01:58.770
So we will get all the rows from the employees and put it in the persons, all the roads from the customers,

01:58.770 --> 02:05.370
from the suppliers and as well from the students and put everything in one big table that holds all

02:05.370 --> 02:09.490
the information about the individuals that we have inside our database.

02:09.490 --> 02:15.770
And now the next step after we combine the data, now we write an SQL query in order to analyze this

02:15.770 --> 02:16.730
new big table.

02:16.730 --> 02:19.930
And the result is going to be presented in their reports.

02:19.930 --> 02:25.210
And now of course, the advantage here is that we have only one SQL query for the data analysis on top

02:25.210 --> 02:28.050
of this table instead of having it four times.

02:28.090 --> 02:33.130
And now if you go and change the logic of the SQL query, it's going to be applied automatically on

02:33.130 --> 02:35.050
all the data that we have in the database.

02:35.050 --> 02:39.610
And we have done already this example where we have combined the data between the employees and customers.

02:39.650 --> 02:43.050
Another scenario where we have to combine data before doing any reporting.

02:43.050 --> 02:49.730
That's sometimes the database developers tend to divide a table one big table into multiple small tables

02:49.730 --> 02:51.530
in order to optimize the performance.

02:51.530 --> 02:57.420
For example, here splitting the orders by the year, we have orders 2022 2023.

02:57.460 --> 02:58.060
Now again here.

02:58.060 --> 03:04.100
If you want to generate a report in order to analyze the orders over the years, over the time, either

03:04.100 --> 03:09.020
you're going to go and make a query for each of those tables, or you're going to go first, combining

03:09.020 --> 03:12.700
all those tables into one table called orders.

03:12.860 --> 03:17.420
So what we're going to do, we're going to use a union between all those tables in order to generate

03:17.460 --> 03:19.700
one central table called the orders.

03:19.780 --> 03:24.060
So all the rows from the first table and or rows from the next table.

03:24.420 --> 03:27.100
The next one and the last one.

03:27.100 --> 03:29.700
So we're going to put everything in one big table.

03:29.700 --> 03:35.700
And once we have the orders we're going to go and write analytical SQL query on top of the orders in

03:35.700 --> 03:37.940
order to generate the report.

03:37.980 --> 03:43.260
So as you can see, it's very important step in order to prepare the data before doing data analysis.

03:43.300 --> 03:43.500
Okay.

03:43.540 --> 03:45.100
So now let's have the following SQL task.

03:45.100 --> 03:48.620
And it says the orders are stored in separate tables.

03:48.620 --> 03:50.980
We have the orders and orders archive.

03:51.020 --> 03:55.350
Now combine all orders data into one report without duplicates.

03:55.390 --> 03:59.870
Okay, so by looking to the task we have to combine two tables orders and orders archive.

03:59.910 --> 04:01.590
So either union or union all.

04:01.750 --> 04:06.310
But since the task says without duplicates that means we have to go with a union.

04:06.310 --> 04:11.430
But now before we combine any data, we have first to understand the content of the orders and the orders

04:11.430 --> 04:14.430
archive in order to map the columns correctly.

04:14.630 --> 04:17.590
So first we have to go and explore the two tables.

04:17.950 --> 04:23.430
So let's start with selecting the data from orders everything semicolon.

04:23.590 --> 04:30.550
And as well from the second table sales orders archive and as well semicolon.

04:30.710 --> 04:32.550
So let's go and execute it.

04:32.550 --> 04:36.030
So now in the output we get two results because we have two separate queries.

04:36.030 --> 04:39.790
The first result is for the orders and the second one is for the orders.

04:39.830 --> 04:40.510
Archive.

04:40.790 --> 04:42.510
Let me just make it a little bit bigger.

04:42.750 --> 04:46.430
And now as you can see we have almost identical tables.

04:46.630 --> 04:49.630
So as you can see we have the order ID, product ID customer ID.

04:50.070 --> 04:52.630
So everything looks like identical.

04:52.670 --> 04:56.670
And of course, we can go and check that using the object Explorer on the left side.

04:56.910 --> 05:00.590
So we have here the orders and those are the columns.

05:00.790 --> 05:07.190
And if you go to the orders archive you can see that we have the exact same columns.

05:07.190 --> 05:12.230
So that means we can go and map all columns from orders with the all columns of orders archive.

05:12.270 --> 05:13.870
So let's go and do that.

05:13.910 --> 05:16.110
So I'm just going to remove all semicolons.

05:16.110 --> 05:18.030
And then we're going to go and use the union.

05:18.030 --> 05:20.070
So now we have everything in one query.

05:20.110 --> 05:21.750
Let's go and execute it.

05:21.790 --> 05:27.630
Now we will get in the output one single result one single table with all the formations from orders

05:27.630 --> 05:28.790
and orders archive.

05:28.830 --> 05:34.030
So we have all orders now in one table and everything currently is matching.

05:34.030 --> 05:35.390
So with that we have solved the task.

05:35.390 --> 05:37.670
We have one result with all orders.

05:37.670 --> 05:38.830
We don't have any duplicates.

05:38.830 --> 05:41.790
Since we are using union and we have combined the data.

05:41.790 --> 05:43.750
But now we have one issue with that.

05:43.950 --> 05:49.310
This solution, this query is quick and dirty and actually it's not following the best practices.

05:49.310 --> 05:55.760
So now the best practices here is to list clearly all the columns in each query without using star.

05:55.800 --> 05:56.080
All right.

05:56.120 --> 05:57.240
So now let's go and do that.

05:57.240 --> 06:01.720
Now we need a list of all columns from the table orders and the table orders archive.

06:01.720 --> 06:05.840
And since we have a lot of columns what we can do we go to Object Explorer.

06:06.000 --> 06:10.640
Right click on the table name and then let's go select the top 1000 rows.

06:10.640 --> 06:11.680
So let's click on that.

06:11.720 --> 06:16.640
And now we're going to get a very simple select statements where we have all the column names from the

06:16.640 --> 06:17.560
table orders.

06:17.960 --> 06:21.880
This is what I usually do if I need all the columns in my select statements.

06:21.880 --> 06:24.880
So let's go and copy it and go back to our query.

06:25.200 --> 06:29.320
Then let's go replace the first star with those columns.

06:29.320 --> 06:34.400
And we're going to do the same thing as well for the orders archive since they have the same names.

06:34.400 --> 06:36.000
So let's go and do that as well.

06:36.000 --> 06:39.880
So let me just make this smaller in order to see the query.

06:40.120 --> 06:46.320
So now we have a select for the table orders with all columns and as well a select with all columns

06:46.320 --> 06:47.760
for the table orders archive.

06:48.050 --> 06:49.850
So let's go and execute it.

06:49.850 --> 06:52.330
And of course now we're going to go and get the same results.

06:52.370 --> 06:54.410
Now you might ask why we are doing this.

06:54.450 --> 06:55.970
Why didn't we stick with the star.

06:55.970 --> 06:56.450
It's quick.

06:56.490 --> 06:57.170
It's simple.

06:57.410 --> 06:59.090
Well for the following reason.

06:59.090 --> 07:01.970
So now currently the status is that everything is matching.

07:01.970 --> 07:04.850
We have 100% identical tables.

07:04.850 --> 07:10.250
But what happened with the time is that we do developments in our solution, and we might go and change

07:10.250 --> 07:12.130
the schema of the table orders.

07:12.130 --> 07:17.170
So we might rename stuff, we might add new columns or maybe switch the columns.

07:17.170 --> 07:22.690
So this means the table order with the time will not be any more identical with the archive.

07:22.810 --> 07:27.770
And this is of course a problem if you are mapping the data blindly using the star.

07:27.810 --> 07:28.930
So now let me show you what I mean.

07:28.930 --> 07:34.770
Let's say that in this table we are developing the orders and we just switch those two columns in the

07:34.770 --> 07:36.690
schema for some reason.

07:37.050 --> 07:40.730
So now we have the product ID first and then the order ID.

07:41.050 --> 07:42.970
So let's go and execute it.

07:43.010 --> 07:45.930
Now if you are using star you will not notice these informations.

07:45.930 --> 07:50.020
formations, but if you are using script, you're gonna see immediately that here we have first the

07:50.020 --> 07:53.500
order ID and then product ID and here we have the opposite.

07:53.500 --> 07:57.660
So it's more clear listing the columns then using the star.

07:57.700 --> 08:03.380
And now as you can see in the output, you can see that we have a problem that here we have order IDs.

08:03.700 --> 08:07.700
And then suddenly we have something like the product ID.

08:07.700 --> 08:11.340
So we're going to have incorrect data which leads to incorrect analysis.

08:11.340 --> 08:15.820
So here are the best practices to not use the star and to clearly list all the columns.

08:15.860 --> 08:21.740
Now one more technique that I usually use once I'm combining data is that I add the source of the data

08:21.740 --> 08:22.740
inside the query.

08:22.740 --> 08:28.140
So what I mean with that, now you can see that we have here two orders with the order ID one.

08:28.180 --> 08:29.100
They are not duplicates.

08:29.100 --> 08:30.940
They are completely different informations.

08:30.940 --> 08:33.860
And that's because they come from different tables.

08:33.860 --> 08:37.460
So what I usually do I go and add the source of each record.

08:37.500 --> 08:42.500
It's really nice information for the analytics for the users to understand where these records come

08:42.500 --> 08:42.940
from.

08:42.940 --> 08:43.940
So how are we going to do that?

08:43.940 --> 08:49.420
We're going to have, for example, on the first column the following word, let's say orders.

08:49.900 --> 08:53.660
And we're going to call it let's say that source table.

08:53.780 --> 08:57.420
And we're going to do the same thing as well in the second query.

08:57.420 --> 08:57.820
Right.

08:57.860 --> 09:00.700
So the source table here is not the orders.

09:00.700 --> 09:02.340
It's the orders archive.

09:02.620 --> 09:08.580
So I'm just adding a static columns to my query in order to see the source of the table.

09:08.580 --> 09:10.700
So now we have here two different values.

09:10.860 --> 09:12.540
And let's go and execute it.

09:12.540 --> 09:16.660
And now you see we have created a new column called Source Table where it has only two values.

09:16.660 --> 09:18.580
We have the orders and the orders archive.

09:18.740 --> 09:21.260
Let's go and sort the data by the order ID.

09:21.500 --> 09:24.340
So order by order ID.

09:24.340 --> 09:25.900
So let's go and execute it.

09:25.900 --> 09:27.740
And now you can see it very clearly.

09:27.740 --> 09:31.260
The first order order ID one comes from the table orders.

09:31.460 --> 09:34.340
And the second one comes from the orders archive.

09:34.340 --> 09:39.140
So this is really nice information that you can add to your data once you are combining multiple tables.

09:39.140 --> 09:43.180
So that's all about this use case on how to combine data between different tables.
